The '''2026 Thurrock Council election''' took place on 7 May 2026 to elect members to [[Thurrock Council]] in [[Essex]], England.<ref>{{Cite web |date=31 January 2023 |title=Election timetable in England |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/election-timetable-in-england/election-timetable-in-england#district-councils2 |access-date=4 September 2024 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (UK)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref> 49 seats were elected, on the same day as the [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|UK's 2026 local elections]].

Originally scheduled for 2025, the election was brought forward by a year to 2026 after the government intervened in the council after it declared bankruptcy, with a new electoral system installed where all 49 seats to the council would be up for a term of four years.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sexton |first=Christine |date=19 December 2024 |title=Reform councillor is critical of plans to cancel May elections |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/reform-councillor-is-critical-of-plans-to-cancel-may-elections-248218 |access-date=5 February 2025 |work=Thurrock Nub News}}</ref> In February 2025, elections in the borough were brought back to 2026, ahead of local government reorganisation and devolution.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Weakley |first=Kirsty |date=5 February 2025 |title=Nine polls cancelled as devo priority programme unveiled |url=https://www.lgcplus.com/politics/devolution-and-economic-growth/breaking-nine-polls-cancelled-as-devo-priority-programme-unveilled-05-02-2025/ |access-date=5 February 2025 |work=Local Government Chronicle}}</ref> The 2026 election was due to be cancelled ahead of local government reorganisation, but in February it was reinstated.<ref>{{cite news |title=Government abandons plans to delay 30 council elections |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c70ne31d884o |access-date=16 February 2026 |work=BBC News |date=16 February 2026}}</ref>

Thurrock Council is soon to be abolished under the plans of the [[English Devolution and Community Empowerment Bill]] and will be merged with Basildon Council to form a new South West Essex Unitary Authority. <ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-03-25 |title=Essex councils to be replaced with five unitaries, says Reed |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9d4p97g341o |access-date=2026-04-11 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== Background ==
The 2026 election will be held under new ward boundaries.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 July 2024 |title=Next stage of consultation for council wards launched |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/news/wards-and-polling-stations/next-stage-of-consultation-for-council-wards-launched |website=Thurrock Council}}</ref> The council is switching to all-out elections, having previously elected by thirds, as a result of a period of government intervention in the running of the council's finances.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.yourthurrock.com/2023/10/05/government-order-all-out-elections-in-thurrock-in-2025/ | title=Government order all out elections in Thurrock in 2025 | date=5 October 2023 }}</ref>

At the last election in 2024, the Labour Party led by John Kent won an overall majority with 27 seats. The Conservatives led by Andrew Jefferies won a total of 13 seats, leaving the council administration and entering opposition. Independent politicians won 9 seats, five of whom were aligned with the Non-Political Alliance of Independent Councillors (NPAIC) led by Neil Speight. By July 2024, eight of the nine independents had joined the alliance. Alex Anderson, the unaffiliated independent, joined Reform UK in September 2024, becoming Reform's first councillor in Thurrock. In October 2024, NPAIC councillor John Allen left the NPAIC to sit as an unaffiliated independent, as he was considering standing for Reform UK in the 2025 council election, leaving the NPAIC with seven independents.

In January 2025, [[Ockendon (ward)|Ockendon]] councillor Ryan Polston of the Labour Party resigned his seat ahead of a career move.<ref>{{Cite news |date=9 January 2025 |title=Labour councillor resigns, borough looks set for intriguing by-election |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/labour-councillor-resigns-borough-looks-set-for-intriguing-by-election-249333 |access-date=3 November 2025 |work=Thurrock Nub News}}</ref> He was followed in March by fellow ward councillor Andrew Jefferies, the leader of the Conservative group, who resigned for personal reasons.<ref>{{Cite news |date=3 March 2025 |title=By-election in Ockendon will have two posts up for grabs after top Tory's resignation |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/by-election-in-ockendon-will-have-two-posts-up-for-grabs-after-top-torys-resignation-253792 |access-date=3 November 2025 |work=Thurrock Nub News}}</ref> Reform UK won both seats at the subsequent by-election in May 2025.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dexter |first=Aimee |date=2 May 2025 |title=Reform UK wins three seats in Essex by-elections |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cewg9p7gx98o |access-date=3 November 2025 |work=BBC News}}</ref>  Ahead of the by-election, Labour leader John Kent resigned from the leadership to make way for his deputy Lynn Worrall ahead of devolution and local government reorganisation negotiations.<ref>{{Cite news |date=28 April 2025 |title=Lynn Worrall takes helm of council's Labour group |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/lynn-worrall-takes-helm-of-councils-labour-group-258591 |access-date=3 November 2025 |work=Thurrock Nub News}}</ref> Later that year, NPAIC members Gary Byrne and Ross Byrne left the group to sit as unaffiliated independents, which left the council with 26 Labour members, 12 Conservatives, five NPAIC members, three unaffiliated independents and three Reform UK members.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Current councillors |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/current-councillors |access-date=3 November 2025 |website=Thurrock Council}}</ref>

== Campaign ==
The Conservatives & Unionist Party, Labour, and Reform UK managed to fill a full slate of 49 candidates in Thurrock. <ref>{{Cite web |title=Statement of Persons Nominated 2026 {{!}} Local elections 2026 {{!}} Thurrock Council |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/local-elections-2026/statement-of-persons-nominated-2026 |access-date=2026-04-11 |website=www.thurrock.gov.uk}}</ref>

The Green Party of England and Wales for the first time, has managed to put at least one candidate forward in every ward. <ref>{{Cite web |last=Reporter |first=Nub News |date=2026-04-10 |title=Candidates announced as election battle begins in earnest in Thurrrock. Old faces disappear but there are some familiar (and controversial) returnees |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/candidates-announced-as-election-battle-begins-in-earnest-in-thurrrock-old-faces-disappear-but-there-are-some-familiar-and-controversial-returnees-292919 |access-date=2026-04-11 |website=Thurrock Nub News |language=en}}</ref>

